  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "2f498341-256d-819f-5ed8-0975a8c99af6",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "6bae343e-55c6-eafc-178d-15005e4d207d",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The workshop in Turiani labeled TANGRAM DAY included working with teachers on developing materials for various teaching purposes including understanding and teaching numeracy, oral literacy, spatial literacy and conceptual literacy. For example, the CPs worked in groups to create a narrative, prepare masks and act out the narrative, all to support the development of skills early reading.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The second workshop took place at the AKU-IED, EA campus and attracted over 80 participants for its event: Taking Making Into the Schools: Embracing the Maker Movement as a Way to Introduce Design Thinking and Innovation. The participants included the Institute's first and second year Master of Education students. The philosophy of the Maker Movement suggests that for children to survive and thrive in the 21st century, they must be adept in using the skills essential in the knowledge age.  These skills include design thinking, problem finding and solving, and creativity.  Incorporating these ideas, the session turned to be a very busy, hands-on, session that was fun and challenging.  ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "These two very stimulating workshops really succeeded in getting CAREG of to a flying, productive and creative start.  Dr Lillian Vikuru, as PI, will lead the project at IED-EA, managing the research and scholarship of teaching activities.  Lillian will spend 3 months at the University of British Columbia with Dr. Susan Crichton later in 2014.  Susan, herself, has recently been honoured with an award in Teaching Excellence and Innovation for her 'maker movement' work at UBC, where she directs the Innovative Learning Centre. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "The Okanagan at the University of British Columbia also shares  how \"'making' could become part of their everyday teaching\", read more ",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "text": "here",
                  "type": "text",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "link",
                      "attrs": {
