    "show_share": true,
    "description": "Can you teach at school with recycled plastic bottles, cardboard paper, plastic lids, and glue? Dr. Lillian Vikiru, Assistant Professor at AKU-IED EA and Dr. Susan Crichton, Visiting Professor to IED from the University of British Columbia, Canada have shown how readily available resources found in communities, even the most marginalized, can be used to develop resources to stimulate young children and their teachers. They conducted two exciting material making workshops to support early years' literacy development for educators one in Turiani, a rural part of Tanzania set in the district of Morogoro - 360 km from Dar es Salaam,  and the other at the Aga Khan University campus in Dar es Salaam. These workshops launched the Canada Africa Research Grant (CAREG) project - Leapfrogging Pedagogical Challenges: Using Appropriate Technology to Foster Innovations in Literacy for the Knowledge Age, a collaboration between the two universities. This project will work with teachers from rural Tanzania to design, create and try out in their own classrooms materials and through the development of innovative teaching and learning resources from locally available materials and technologies, prototypes for work with other teachers will be developed.",
